{# ── Short answer ── #}
<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> yes, many visa holders can buy a home in Australia. What you can buy, and how much a lender will lend, comes down to your visa. Permanent residents are treated much like citizens. Temporary residents need federal approval (FIRB, explained below) and can't buy an established home until 30 June 2029. On top of that, most states and territories charge foreign buyers extra stamp duty, and some charge extra land tax every year. Partner visa holders buying with a citizen or PR spouse often have the easiest path.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Everyone's situation is different, so this is general information, not advice.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Federal vs state ── #}
<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>Federal rules and state rules: what's the difference?</h2>
    <p>Two layers of rules apply.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Federal rules</strong> are the same everywhere in Australia. They decide whether you need approval to buy and what type of property you can buy.</li>
      <li><strong>State and territory rules</strong> decide the extra costs. Each state sets its own foreign buyer stamp duty, land tax surcharge and first home buyer help, and each has its own test for who counts as foreign.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So you can be fine federally and still pay a state surcharge, or the other way around.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Foreign person ── #}
<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>Are you a "foreign person"?</h2>
    <p>For federal property rules you're generally a foreign person if you're not an Australian citizen, not a permanent resident, and not a New Zealand citizen on a special category visa (subclass 444). Temporary visa holders, including most bridging visa holders, are foreign persons.</p>
    <p>States use their own definitions. Most treat permanent residents as not foreign. NSW adds a test based on days spent in Australia. The state table below shows the differences.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── What is FIRB? ── #}
<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>What is FIRB?</h2>
    <p>FIRB is the Foreign Investment Review Board. It sits within the federal Treasury and screens foreign purchases of Australian property and businesses, advising the Treasurer. For homes, the ATO runs the process: you lodge the application and pay the fee through the ATO.</p>
    <p>If you're a foreign person, including a temporary resident, you generally need FIRB approval before you buy a home anywhere in Australia, whatever the price. Permanent residents, citizens and NZ citizens on a 444 visa don't need it.</p>
    <p>Approval means the government doesn't object to you buying that kind of property. It usually comes with conditions. For example, vacant land has to be built on within four years, and foreign owners have to lodge a vacancy return with the ATO each year. Your contract should be conditional on getting approval.</p>
  </div>
</section>

