{# ── FIRB cost ── #}
<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>How much does FIRB approval cost?</h2>
    <p>The fee is tiered by price and changes each year. For new homes or vacant land bought between 1 July 2026 and 30 June 2027, the ATO lists $15,600 for a property up to $1 million and $31,300 up to $2 million. Check the ATO's current table before you sign.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Established homes ── #}
<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>Can temporary residents buy an established home?</h2>
    <p>Not right now. From 1 April 2025, foreign persons, including temporary residents, are banned from buying established homes anywhere in Australia. The 2026-27 Budget extended the ban to 30 June 2029. Older articles still quote March 2027. That's out of date.</p>
    <p>With FIRB approval, you can still buy:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a new or near-new home</li>
      <li>an off-the-plan apartment or house</li>
      <li>vacant land to build on</li>
    </ul>
    <p>There are narrow exceptions for big redevelopments that add 20 or more homes, and for some employer-provided worker housing. They aren't a way for a household to buy an established home. Knocking down one house and building one new one still counts as buying an established home.</p>
  </div>
</section>

