{# ── Worked example: NSW ── #}
<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>Worked example: NSW</h2>
    <p>NSW shows how the state layer works, and it has one test that catches a lot of people out.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Surcharge purchaser duty: 9%</strong> of the price, on top of normal stamp duty. Revenue NSW's own example is a $1 million purchase attracting $90,000 in surcharge duty plus $39,187 in standard duty.</li>
      <li><strong>Surcharge land tax: 5%</strong> of the land value every year. There's no tax-free threshold, so it applies even when normal land tax doesn't.</li>
      <li><strong>The 200-day test.</strong> In NSW, a permanent resident only avoids the 9% if they've been in Australia for at least 200 days in the 12 months before the contract. If not, they can still be exempt by living in the home as their main residence for 200 days in the 12 months after the contract. Partner visa holders (309/820) can use the same tests.</li>
      <li><strong>Land tax uses a different count.</strong> A PR needs 200 days in Australia in the calendar year. Spend 165 days or more overseas in a year and the 5% can apply.</li>
      <li><strong>Refunds.</strong> If you paid the surcharge at exchange and you're no longer a foreign person by the date the property transfers to you, for example because PR came through, you may be able to apply for a refund. It isn't automatic.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Other states work differently. Queensland, for example, fixes your status on the contract date, so getting PR before settlement doesn't remove its surcharge.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Buying with a partner ── #}
<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>Can buying with your partner avoid FIRB?</h2>
    <p>Yes, if it's done properly. If you're a temporary resident and you buy as <strong>joint tenants</strong> with your spouse or de facto partner who's an Australian citizen, a permanent resident or an eligible NZ citizen, the purchase is exempt from FIRB. That means the established-home ban doesn't apply either.</p>
    <p>The details matter:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>It has to be joint tenants. Tenants in common doesn't qualify.</li>
      <li>Your partner's citizenship or PR has to already be granted.</li>
      <li>Tell your conveyancer before the contract is drawn up.</li>
      <li>It doesn't switch off state surcharges, although some states have their own exemptions for couples buying a home to live in. See the table.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Most lenders will want both of you on the loan.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Trusts and nominees ── #}
<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>What about trusts, nominees or a relative buying for you?</h2>
    <p>Steer clear. You'll see these sold online as loopholes. They aren't.</p>
    <p>If a citizen buys a home that's really for a foreign relative, FIRB looks through to the real owner. The ATO is targeting this, and the results can include forced sale orders and penalties. A company or trust with a foreign beneficiary is usually treated as a foreign person anyway, which brings FIRB and state surcharges straight back in.</p>
    <p>A family guarantor can help with a loan, but it doesn't change anyone's FIRB status.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── First home schemes ── #}
<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <h2>Can visa holders use first home buyer schemes?</h2>
    <p>Mostly no. The federal 5% Deposit Scheme is for citizens and permanent residents only, and every applicant on the loan has to qualify. Help to Buy is for citizens only.</p>
    <p>State first home grants and stamp duty concessions generally need at least one buyer to be a citizen or permanent resident. Even where a concession applies, the foreign buyer's share can still attract the state surcharge. The table above shows each state.</p>
  </div>
</section>

